Investigate a service from requirements to operating evidence (@engineering-foundation-study: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Review-engineering-claims-from-function-to-continuing-service.lingua#r_0226B0839C667959908DA37D5B") { r_0154834E27D6627BE8DA2ED738
Bring the preceding questions together in a bounded original study of the hypothetical paper-card service. The aim is a defensible engineering argument: a stated use, measurable requirements, alternatives, explicit models, checked calculations and a plan for evidence. This is a paper and computational investigation, not a report of an operated production line.

**Read:** revisit the selected NASA requirements, realization, interface and configuration passages, and [Slocum's introductory design discussion](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/FUNdaMENTALS-of-Design-Slocum.lingua#r_58Q6VQJ3FXXGXRBJ3DK0AVC3WQ). Use each source to question an actual decision in your study. Record which passages you read and where their institutional or mechanical context differs from your example. Do not substitute a book's authority for the missing observation of your proposed design.

Use the ten-hour model with A's rate 120/h, availability 0.9 and accepted fraction 0.95; B's rate 100/h, availability 0.95 and accepted fraction 0.98. Begin with no intermediate inventory or rework. Independently obtain upstream capacity 1,026, downstream input capacity 950 and final acceptable bound 931. If both stages use those full interval capacities, reconcile 1,080 initial blanks with 54 A rejects, 19 B rejects, 931 final acceptable cards and 76 intermediates. Explain why final acceptable output divided by all blanks processed by A is not the completed two-stage yield when unfinished inventory remains.

A coordinated aggregate alternative processes only 1,000 blanks at A. It produces 950 acceptable intermediates and 50 rejects. B then gives 931 acceptable cards and 19 rejects, with zero net buffer growth. Over A's nine operating hours, this requires an average operating rate of 1,000/9 = 111.111… per hour, below its stated maximum of 120. The full count is 1,000 = 50 + 19 + 931. The completed-stream accepted fraction is 0.95 × 0.98 = 0.931 under the stated fixed-fraction assumptions. Zero net growth still does not imply that a zero-capacity buffer or any arbitrary schedule will work.

Construct a separate tiny scheduling counterexample using perfect acceptance and zero transfer delay. Two time slots each last one hour. A and B can each process at most one item in a slot when available; B may process only items present at the start of its slot. Both start with no items. If B is available only in slot 1 and A only in slot 2, both have one unit of aggregate capacity but final output is zero. If A is available in slot 1 and B in slot 2, final output is one. The same aggregate capacities admit different outcomes because their ordering differs. State the within-slot convention; changing it changes the model.

Use the shared stock-trace function to test buffer counts for an explicit arrival/departure event sequence. It detects bound violations in a supplied sequence. It does not decide which machine is available, derive a schedule, or prove that a proposed departure is physically possible beyond the supplied count contract. Check the tiny counterexample independently by enumerating the two slots on paper before automating it.

Compare the four candidate designs in the alternatives lesson. Add an operating interruption and one interface change, then revise the relevant predictions and evidence plan. Choose a candidate provisionally or explain why the evidence is insufficient. The choice must refer to the intended service, not only to the highest nominal rate. Finally ask a second reader to find an assumption that could reverse your conclusion and revise the study in response.

**Deliver:** requirements and reasons; boundary/interface diagrams; model definitions and units; independent calculations; the two schedules; a conditional alternative comparison; evidence needed for product, interface and service claims; and a handover/recovery account. Separate assumed data, derived results, proposed observations and observations actually performed. This first investigation prepares deeper engineering study; it does not establish a production-ready design or complete any engineering discipline.

**Shared event model:** [the original stock-trace program and independent prefix-sum checks](../../computer-science/core/Investigate-a-stock-trace-with-independent-computational-evidence.lingua#r_06AE2B0429294B062E80CC37F6). **Shared assumptions:** [Investigate a stock model and defend its alternatives](../../mathematics/core/Investigate-a-stock-model-and-its-alternatives.lingua#r_0149F4044C03C0F4316E216838).

**Author verification:** exact rational arithmetic passed 857 comparisons, including 676 capacity cases checked against feasible-throughput enumeration and 81 two-slot schedules checked against an independently derived result. The stated count balances, intervals, tradeoff examples, limiting and changed-parameter cases also passed. These are checks of the original teaching examples, not observations of a physical line.

**Continue:** [Review engineering claims from function to continuing service](Review-engineering-claims-from-function-to-continuing-service.lingua#r_0226B0839C667959908DA37D5B).

